From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivo Nunes <ivo@nunes.uk>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 19:37:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Scene: send nothing the servers already have after
 World3D.shift()

RenderingServer.scenario_shift() and PhysicsServer3D.space_shift()
move a world's servers' state at once; World3D.shift() calls both and
keeps a running total of each. The nodes are then moved to match by
moving what they hang from, and would otherwise send every new
transform to the servers one by one, undoing the saving.

VisualInstance3D and CollisionObject3D now remember the transform they
last sent and the world's shift then. After a shift they don't send a
transform the server already has (the old one moved by the shift); a
node that has also moved for real sends as before. Where a physics
engine couldn't shift its space, its total doesn't change and
collision objects send as before. update_server_transform() resends one
as it is (and a body's debug shapes), for nodes the servers moved while
the node stayed where it was (outside what moved, or top-level). Nothing in Godot's own nodes
uses VisualInstance3D's identity transform mode, which this doesn't
cover.

No upstream issue yet.

Test: a world node's meshes and bodies end up drawn and solid where
their nodes are after World3D.shift() and moving the world node by the
same amount, as do nodes outside the world and top-level nodes (put back
with update_server_transform()), and a node moved for real in the same
frame. On the city, a shift with Purr's WorldOrigin costs about 30 ms
over normal frames, down from 55 to 70.
